From: Deividas Nastajus <16858119+dns54@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 19:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 26/29] soc/intel/skylake: Give the PCH-H GPIO controller its
 own ACPI ID

The GPIO device always has _HID INT344B, Intel's ID for the PCH-LP
controller. On PCH-H the communities hold other pads and the pad
config lock registers sit at 0x90 instead of 0xa0, so the OS drives
them with the wrong layout. Linux, for example, binds its Sunrise
Point LP data to a B250 and reads every pad's lock state from the
wrong register.

Use the IDs Intel's reference code and OEM firmware use: INT3451 on
100-series PCH-H and INT345D on 200-series PCH-H, told apart by the
LPC/eSPI device ID (0xa1xx and 0xa2xx). The three memory resources
already follow the PCH-H community order Linux expects (0xaf, 0xae,
0xac).

Signed-off-by: Deividas Nastajus <16858119+dns54@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 src/soc/intel/skylake/acpi/gpio.asl | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/soc/intel/skylake/acpi/gpio.asl b/src/soc/intel/skylake/acpi/gpio.asl
index 1d22b78f66..cdbde05996 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/skylake/acpi/gpio.asl
+++ b/src/soc/intel/skylake/acpi/gpio.asl
@@ -4,7 +4,25 @@
 
 Device (GPIO)
 {
+#if CONFIG(SKYLAKE_SOC_PCH_H)
+	/* The LPC/eSPI device ID: 0xa1xx on 100-series PCHs, 0xa2xx on 200-series */
+	OperationRegion (LPCI, SystemMemory, CONFIG_ECAM_MMCONF_BASE_ADDRESS + 0xf8000, 4)
+	Field (LPCI, WordAcc, NoLock, Preserve)
+	{
+		Offset (2),
+		LDID, 16,
+	}
+
+	Method (_HID)
+	{
+		If ((LDID >> 8) == 0xa2) {
+			Return ("INT345D")
+		}
+		Return ("INT3451")
+	}
+#else
 	Name (_HID, "INT344B")
+#endif
 	Name (_UID, 1)
 	Name (_DDN, "GPIO Controller")
 
